#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Check the bundled theme files: tokens, colours and contrast.

    script/unaligned/check-themes              every assets/themes/*/*.json
    script/unaligned/check-themes FILE...      these files only
    script/unaligned/check-themes -v           also list every ratio of the other bundled themes
    script/unaligned/check-themes -q           failures and the verdict only

Output: one line per finding, a count line, then PASS or FAIL. Exit status 0 or 1.

    FAIL <theme> <check> <value> < <target>    a ratio under its target
    FAIL <theme> unset <token>                 a required token left unset (or null)
    FAIL <theme> color <token> ...             a colour string the app cannot parse
    FAIL <file> json ...                       a file the app cannot load
    WARN ...                                   same checks, not counted as failures
    NOTE ...                                   a deliberate exception (see below)

What is checked

  Every theme
    - The file is strict JSON. Comments are understood by this script but are a failure for
      files under assets/themes: the app loads bundled themes with serde_json, which rejects
      them (the theme would be skipped with one log line). Duplicate keys fail everywhere.
    - The shape the app needs: family name, author, themes; theme name, appearance, style.
    - Every colour string parses the way gpui does (#rgb, #rgba, #rrggbb, #rrggbbaa). The app
      treats a colour it cannot parse as unset, silently.
    - No two themes share a name (the registry is keyed by name: the later one would win).

  Themes whose family name starts with "Unaligned" (failures)
    - No unknown key in "style" (the app ignores unknown keys: a typo is an unset token).
    - No required token left unset or null. Required = every colour and status key of the
      schema, the new tokens (accent.background, accent.hover, accent.foreground,
      border.strong, text.faint), "corners": "square", seven accents, a complete local player,
      and the syntax names of REQUIRED_SYNTAX. Not required: the tokens whose fallback is
      right (NOT_REQUIRED), the vim.* mode colours and the deprecated aliases.
    - The contrast targets of the design system (the numbers are the constants below).
      WCAG 2.1 ratios; a colour with alpha is blended on its real ground first.
      The grounds are chrome (`background`), editor (`editor.background`) and raised
      (`elevated_surface.background`).

        text, editor.foreground, terminal foreground              7    on its grounds
        text.muted, text.faint, text.accent, link_text.hover      4.5  on the three grounds
        text.placeholder, text.disabled                           3    on the three grounds
        border.focused, panel and pane focused borders            3
        error, warning, info, success, created, modified,
          deleted, conflict, renamed                              4.5  on chrome and editor
        hint, predictive                                          4.5  on the editor
        hidden, ignored                                           3    on chrome and editor
        version_control.added/modified/deleted/conflict           3    on the editor
        editor.line_number                                        3    (active number 4.5)
        every syntax colour and every accent                      4.5  on the editor
        text and every syntax colour                              4.5  on the selection
        text                                                      4.5  on search matches and
                                                                       on diff added/deleted fills
        terminal.ansi.* normal and bright                         4.5  on the terminal ground
        terminal.ansi.dim_*                                       3
        accent.foreground                                         4.5  on the fill and its hover
        editor.active_line.background against the editor          1.05 ("visible")
        element.selected against the panel                        1.2  ("visible")
        border / border.strong against their grounds              1.2 / 1.5 (see exception 1)

  Other bundled themes (One, Ayu, Gruvbox): the same ratios as warnings, on the tokens they
  set: one summary line per theme, every ratio with -v. Unset tokens are not reported.

Deliberate exceptions, printed as NOTE and never counted

  1. Structure lines. `border` (about 1.3:1) and `border.strong` (about 1.7:1) are under the
     3:1 that WCAG 1.4.11 asks of the outline of a control. The 1px lines between regions are
     decoration and a brand choice; inputs and outlined buttons use `border.strong`, and the
     Unaligned Contrast theme is the one that passes in full. The lines must still be there:
     `border` under 1.2:1 or `border.strong` under 1.5:1 fails.
  2. The brand red fill with white text. #ffffff on #e5322d is 4.35:1, under the 4.5:1 of
     body text (about 5.2:1 on its hover #cf2a25, which is darker than the fill). These are the
     brand's own colours (the site's primary button); labels on the fill are medium weight.
     Only this exact trio is excepted: any other accent fill must reach 4.5:1 with its
     foreground, at rest and on hover.

Two targets of the table are applied with a twist, both explained where they are coded:
comments, hints and predictions on the selection (warning between 3 and 4.5, see
FAINT_SYNTAX), and the ANSI colour that plays the ground (black on dark themes; white, or
black in themes that swap them, on light ones) is not measured against it.

Python 3 standard library only.
"""

